A||||9||olliver||Request For Slammed Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||olliver||11-07-2001||12:09 PM||||You mentioned that you have converted to the "big brake" upgrade.<P>Would it be possible to give a list of all the parts you used?<P>Thanx<BR>Olliver||||reg||1
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||11-07-2001||01:43 PM||||If I were to do it again , I would just buy the kit from archie. It will take out a lot of Guess work and you know it will be right. <BR>While it may seem like it's expensive at $1600 US , It will cost the same amount canadian to do it yourself with many trips to the parts store. (I'm up to about $2500 Canadian)<P>here's the instructions<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus/fieroman/walt/walt.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ipass.net/~fieroman/terranexus/fieroman/walt/walt.htm</A> <P>they seem straight forward , but trust me when I say their not. There is quite a bit of info missing. The guy who did the conversion said he spent $600 , I would like to know how. Unless your a machinist it will cost WAYYYYY more than that. The brackets alone for the calipers are $500 Canadian.<P>If you do decide to go it alone let me know. I have a few things I would have changed to make the swap better. Not really performance things , but just for ease of installation.<BR>If you got archies kit it comes with insturctions ..far more detailed than whats on that page. You don't need many tools. The only thing I had to buy was an Open Shoe grinder for $50 from Crappy tire.<P>Other than that basic handtools , a propane torch to heat up some bolts , liquid wrench , and some bandaids. With a kit and tools on hand it could be done in an afternoon.<P>The bracket CAD drawings are on that page BUT I found I had to get the opening on each bracket machined a bit to clear casting imperfections in the Calipers. There very tight tolerances.<P>It's a great conversion and I reccomend it to anyone. <P>Bill of Materials: <P>4 each   Raybestos P/N 7993 11.260" Vented Rotors (rear, 89-94 Chrysler LeBaron) <BR>1 each  CarQuest  P/N 18-4071 Front Caliper, rebuilt, 82-89 Chevrolet Camaro <BR>1 each  CarQuest  P/N 18-4072 Front Caliper, rebuilt, 82-89 Chevrolet Camaro <BR>1 each  CarQuest  P/N 18-4138 Rear Caliper, rebuilt, 80-85 Cadillac <BR>1 each  CarQuest  P/N 18-4139 Rear Caliper, rebuilt, 80-85 Cadillac <BR>   (These calipers must be identified with casting Nos. 020 and 021) <BR>8 each  Caliper bolts <BR>2 sets  CarQuest  P/N D-154 Disc Pads (Front and rear are same) <BR>1 each  CarQuest  P/N 2218 Master Cylinder, (92-94 Chev/GMC Blazer/Jimmy) Aluminum, 1.125" bore <BR>4 each  3/16" X 24" Teflon stainless steel braided brake hoses.  (Stock hoses are too short) <BR>4 each   Earls P/N 600773 .400" Banjo fittings for above <BR>4 each  Earls P/N 989534 Female 10mm X 1 adapter ends for above <BR>4 each   1/4" AN-type rubber insulated clamps (to secure brake hoses) <BR>10 each Dorman P/N 610-323 Wheel studs, 12M1.50, X 55mm X 12.67mm knurl diameter wheel studs for front hubs. <BR>2 each   Caliper adapters for front, (burned, drilled and tapped 3/8" steel plate) <BR>2 each   Caliper adapters for rear, (burned, drilled and tapped 3/8" steel plate) <BR>4 each  12M1.50 X 40mm Hex head cap screws for front adapters. <BR>8 each   12mm or 1/2" Split Lock washers for 12 mm bolts <BR>4 each   12M1.50 X 25mm Hex head cap screws for rear adapters. <BR>4 each  1" Round X .500" X .435" thick spacers for front caliper adapters. <BR>4 each  .060" thick SAE flat washers for rear caliper spacers. <BR>2 each  E-brake cable housing stops (burned and drilled 1/4" plate) <BR>1 quart  SAE 3 or better Brake Fluid <BR>1 each  12M1.50 Starting tap <BR>1 each   27/64 High Speed Twist Drill <P>Re-used Materials <P>4 each  Banjo fitting bolts from Fiero calipers <BR>2 each  Front hub assemblies, modified with the disc rotors machined off to leave hub flange approximately 5/8" thick. <BR>2 each  Fiero E-brake cable return springs. <BR>1 lot  Fiero E-brake cables. <P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, Big Bore TB, 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pheagey||11-07-2001||02:15 PM||||   I would so pick that amp up from you if my car wasn't in dire need of tires (and I sdo mean dire: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011604.html)." TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011604.html).</A> <BR>  Sorry :wink: maybe anmother time...<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed: Status: destiny unknown.<BR>Broken down: 3 times<BR>Broken into: 2  times (w/i 2 days).<BR>Killed: '97 Civic LX Auto,<BR>Killed by: '93 Civic EX 5 speed,<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects.com/car.html (B4 vandalism)||||reg||1
Z||000003||VISCERAL||11-07-2001||03:12 PM||||BTW, you can get those front spacers at ace hardware, look in the bolt, washer area and look for steel collars, that is 1"x.5".435" or 1"x1/2"x7/16". it is steel and it is perfect. you could locktite the setscrew to lock it in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||11-07-2001||07:30 PM||||Has anybody tried this setup on the 88?  After reading through it, it seems the only thing that would be different is the caliper adapters.  The rotors would have to have a little different hole sizes machined, I'm assuming, but that's easy enough to measure out.  The adapter brackets would have to be measured out, again, not a big deal, easy to measure out and draw my own cad.<P>Basically, I could use this as a parts reference, to know a good source of cheap, easy to find parts that have the performance we want.  I was thinking of doing a swap directly copying a car like a Corvette, but perhaps that's not necessary to research.  The biggest bonus to this setup described is the already researched master cylinder.  I'm unsure how much work it would take to find a usable master cylinder for a setup like on a Corvette.<P>I'm getting 17s, and I'm wondering if I could find a bigger rotor to fit in there, that is used on a car with 17" wheels from the factory.<P>Anybody done anything like this, or used this setup described on an 88?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||avengador1||11-07-2001||07:39 PM||||Remember if you go to bigger brakes to check and see if your spare tire still fits. It's very embarrasing to get a flat tire and not be able to use your spare. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||11-09-2001||07:21 PM||||BUMP...hoping somebody might have some pointers for 88 or some info on swapping entire setups.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||11-09-2001||09:24 PM||||No one has ever tried it on an 88 to my knowledge. If you had a cad program and some engineering experience you could do it. The caliper adaptors are the part you don't want to Screw up. there worth in the $250 to $300 US range<P>As for the spare tire..won't fit. Most people have to Grind the calipers to get them to fit on 16's.<P>Run Flat technology , or just do what I do and don't get a flat<BR>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000008||onfire||11-14-2001||05:44 AM||||I am sending a moneyorder to Archie this friday for the adapters. I have a friend that owns his own shop. I gave him the list of parts I would need. It came over $1000 dollars with core charges. If you returned the cores, it came to $607 and some change. The only thing we didn't get a price on was stainless steel braided brake hoses. So we figured $880.  Not including brake lines and anything we might forget, or break.<P>Anyway Slammed Fiero I would like to here the idea's on how to make the swap better. As my plan of having wheels, tires, and better brakes by the end of the year, is going smoothly. Almost too smooooth. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> And I would hate to mess something up.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/onfiresigtoo.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||onfire||11-14-2001||05:47 AM||||Also do you have a picture of what the front rotor looks like? After you machined them into hubs. Could you post it here or email it to me.||||reg||1
